  "grammar": {
    "title": "6A: Passive — is / are (the simple way)",
    "explanation": "Sometimes we do not say who does the action. We say **is** or **are** instead.\n\nUse **is** for one thing. Use **are** for many things.\n\nThe baby **is** happy. One baby, so we use **is**.\n\nThe boys **are** tired. Many boys, so we use **are**.\n\nThe form is very simple. It is **is** or **are** + the verb in past form, like opened, cooked, or closed.",
    "examples": [
      "*The baby *is* happy.* (one baby)",
      "*The boys *are* tired.* (many boys)",
      "*The food *is* hot, and the people *are* hungry.* (food and people)",
      "*She *is* sad today.* (one person)",
      "*The doors *are* closed.* (many doors)"
    ],
    "practice": [
      {
        "q": "One thing: ___ the door open? (Is or Are?)",
        "a": "Is"
      },
      {
